+# The Daily Grind — account security
+
+A responsive account experience with real WebAuthn passkey registration, discoverable sign-in, passkey management, and reauthentication. The initial screen is an explicitly labeled example account; its example devices are never accepted as credentials. Create an account or sign in from the avatar to use real account data.
+
+## Setup
+
+Requires Node.js 22.13+ (Node.js 24 recommended) and pnpm.
+
+```sh
+pnpm install
+pnpm build
+pnpm start
+```
+
+The application listens on `PORT` (default `11222`). Set `APP_ORIGIN` to the exact public origin, such as `https://coffee.example.com`. WebAuthn requires HTTPS except on localhost. The default origin is `http://localhost:11222` (or the configured port). A production reverse proxy must preserve the browser's Origin header. Changing your domain will make credentials from the old domain unusable.
+
+`DATABASE_PATH` defaults to `data/grind.sqlite`. Persist and protect this file and its SQLite WAL files. All account and passkey metadata are stored here. Browser assets are built to `public/app.js`; no external JavaScript service is required. Fonts and the coffee photo use Google Fonts and Unsplash.
+
+## Account flows
+
+- **Register:** Add a passkey → enter name, email identifier, and passkey name → verify through the device's native WebAuthn prompt. The account is created only after successful attestation verification.
+- **Sign in:** avatar → Sign in with a passkey. Discoverable credentials support accounts without entering an email address. An optional backup password is also supported.
+- **Manage:** add, rename, or remove credentials from the security page. Every UI operation asks for identity confirmation. The server enforces a five-minute verification window. Removing the last credential is blocked unless a backup password exists.
+- **Reauthenticate:** verify with an existing account passkey, or a previously configured backup password. A password-only account can add passkeys after password reauthentication.
+- **Backup:** set a password of 12–128 characters from Manage password. Password changes revoke other active sessions. Removal of a passkey revokes other sessions established with that credential.
+
+Emails identify accounts and are **not verified**. There is no email-based account recovery. Users must retain a synced passkey, second passkey, or backup password. Shopping and café navigation are informational placeholders; this implementation covers account security.
+
+## Security and deployment
+
+The server verifies signatures, origins, RP IDs, user verification, credential ownership, and signature counters using [SimpleWebAuthn](https://simplewebauthn.dev/docs/packages/server). Challenges are bound to sessions and purposes, consumed once, and expire after five minutes. Session tokens are random, hashed in the database, rotated at authentication, and expire after 24 hours. Cookies use HttpOnly, SameSite=Strict, and Secure on HTTPS. Mutations require same-origin JSON requests. Passwords use salted scrypt hashes. Database statements are parameterized.
+
+A per-session request limiter and per-account password-login limiter are included. Before exposing publicly, configure trusted edge/IP rate limits to cover anonymous session creation, backups, monitoring, and the site's privacy/support policy. This single-process SQLite application is intended for a single instance; in-memory rate limits reset on restart.
+
+## Automated checks
+
+```sh
+pnpm check
+pnpm test
+pnpm build
+```
+
+Tests run directly against the request handler with an in-memory database and a cryptographic test authenticator. They create real ES256 signatures to exercise successful verification and reject forged credentials. They do not start a server, import the server entrypoint, or use a browser. Physical authenticator/browser compatibility is not manually verified.
